IBDC Research: Free On-Device OCR and License Plate Recognition

Project: Intelligent Bicycle Detection and Communication (IBDC)
Research date: November 1, 2026
Status: Technology assessment; implementation and device benchmarks not yet completed

1. Objective

Evaluate free technologies capable of extracting text, especially vehicle license plate characters, from incident photographs collected by IBDC’s bicycle-mounted ESP32 device. The preferred solution must run locally on the user’s iOS or Android phone, without uploading incident photos to a paid cloud OCR service.

IBDC currently uses Expo / React Native / TypeScript, BLE to communicate with the ESP32, protobuf messages to transfer events and image chunks, and local SQLite storage for incidents and photos. The intended workflow is analysis of transferred, saved images, not necessarily live camera video.

2. Important distinction: OCR vs. ALPR

• OCR (optical character recognition) reads text in an image. Google ML Kit, Apple Vision, PaddleOCR and Tesseract are examples. These tools may read plate characters if the plate is sufficiently visible, but they do not necessarily locate the plate or distinguish it from other writing.
• ALPR / ANPR (automatic license plate recognition) typically combines plate detection (find and crop the license plate) with plate OCR (transcribe the characters). A vehicle detector is not automatically a license plate detector.
• A practical first prototype can run general OCR over a saved photo and show candidate strings for human review. A stronger system uses a plate detector, plate crop, plate OCR, and confidence/validation logic.

Crucial limitation: License plates captured from a moving bicycle can be small, angled, obscured or motion-blurred. No OCR system can reliably recover characters that are absent from the pixels.

