4. Detailed findings

A. Google ML Kit Text Recognition v2 — recommended initial implementation

Official documentation:

• ML Kit overview
• Text recognition: Android
• Text recognition: iOS

Strengths

• Supported by Google as an on-device mobile SDK; OCR inference is usable offline.
• Free-to-use SDK APIs with no per-image cloud OCR billing.
• Works on Android and iOS via native SDKs.
• Returns detected text and geometry that can help draw overlays or filter candidates.
• Good match for processing one photo after its BLE transfer has completed.

Limitations

• General OCR, not a ready-made ALPR product: scene signs, vehicle branding and street markings may be recognized along with the plate.
• Recognition depends heavily on photo sharpness, illumination, angle and pixel density.
• Google’s guidance recommends characters approximately 16 × 16 pixels or larger in the input image.
• The integration needs a native React Native package or custom module; not supported directly by ordinary Expo Go.

React Native integration research: react-native-vision-camera-mlkit documents an Expo config plugin and static-image processing, but its own versions/platform support and compatibility with IBDC’s installed React Native, Expo and camera versions must be validated before selecting it. It is a Vision Camera integration, which may be unnecessary overhead for IBDC’s stored-image workflow. An image-URI OCR wrapper or small native module might be simpler.

Recommendation: Start here and benchmark on IBDC photos before undertaking a dedicated ALPR pipeline.

B. Apple Vision — iOS-only alternative

Official documentation:

• Recognizing text in images
• RecognizeTextRequest

Strengths

• Text recognition is performed on-device, with offline support.
• Native Apple framework provides fast and accurate recognition modes.
• No separately hosted OCR service or model download required for the basic API.
• Particularly attractive for IBDC’s iPhone testing.

Limitations

• iOS-only; an Android-specific implementation is necessary.
• Swift-to-TypeScript integration or a third-party React Native bridge is required.
• General OCR still requires plate candidate selection and review.

Recommendation: Use if ML Kit integration proves difficult on iOS, or compare Apple Vision against ML Kit on the same stored incident photos.

C. FastPlateOCR — specialized license plate reader

Sources:

• GitHub
• Documentation
• Model reference
• PyPI license metadata

Strengths

• Specifically designed to recognize license plate characters.
• Provides pretrained OCR models and ONNX inference support.
• Documentation discusses export possibilities for mobile-friendly formats, including Core ML / TFLite / ONNX.
• Python package metadata declares an MIT license.

Limitations

• Requires an already cropped plate; not a full photo-to-license-plate pipeline by itself.
• Python example code will not run directly inside a React Native phone app.
• Need to verify licenses and redistribution terms of the exact downloaded model weights, not just the library.
• A native runtime and preprocessing/postprocessing implementation are required; mobile speed and app-size impact are unmeasured.

Recommendation: Test it first on cropped plate images on a development computer, then decide whether a model export and on-device port are justified.

D. FastALPR — end-to-end plate detection and recognition

Sources:

• GitHub
• PyPI

Strengths

• Combines plate detection and OCR; better conceptual match for un-cropped IBDC photos.
• ONNX-based, modular detector/recognizer architecture.
• Python package metadata declares MIT licensing.

Limitations

• Primarily a Python solution; cannot simply install the pip package into Expo React Native.
• Porting the detector and recognizer to Android/iOS requires inference runtime integration and matching preprocessing / decoding.
• Detector and OCR model weights, training data and runtime dependencies each need separate license checks.
• On-device performance, total model size and iOS support need benchmarking.

Recommendation: Treat FastALPR as a promising proof-of-concept and architecture reference for future dedicated ALPR, not the quickest app integration.

E. PaddleOCR — adaptable open-source OCR

Sources:

• Official repository
• Official text detection models
• License

Strengths

• Established Apache-2.0 OCR project with mobile-oriented and tiny model options.
• Separate text detection and recognition components provide customization opportunities.
• No per-image fee for self-run inference.

Limitations

• More mobile deployment work than ML Kit.
• General text detection is not the same as license plate detection.
• Conversion/runtime, memory use, model version and specific weight licenses require validation.

Recommendation: Secondary option if ML Kit is not accurate enough or customization becomes necessary.

F. Tesseract OCR — conventional baseline

Source: Official Tesseract user manual

Strengths: Apache-2.0 open-source OCR engine, long history and configurable text recognition.
Limitations: requires mobile native integration and reliable preprocessing/cropping; lacks plate detection.
Recommendation: Benchmark against ML Kit on cropped plates if needed; not the default choice for the React Native app.

5. Expo / React Native implications

The IBDC app already uses native BLE functionality, so a custom Expo development build is appropriate for on-device OCR. Ordinary Expo Go cannot load arbitrary third-party native libraries. Expo explicitly documents this limitation: Expo FAQ and custom native code guide.

Proposed integration approach:

1. Continue receiving photo chunks over BLE and assembling/storing the resulting image as IBDC already does.
2. Obtain a local image URI after transfer completion and SQLite persistence.
3. Add a TypeScript TextRecognitionService abstraction that accepts an incident image URI and returns recognized text, bounding regions, and optional confidence values.
4. For the first implementation, connect a verified static-image ML Kit wrapper or a local Expo native module. Avoid introducing live-camera infrastructure solely to analyze stored photos.
5. Run OCR asynchronously after image receipt, or on demand from IncidentDetail, rather than blocking BLE writes, image assembly or UI navigation.
6. Save extracted text separately from the original image, along with the recognition engine, version, analysis timestamp and review status.
7. Display “Possible plate text”, never a definitive plate match unless the user confirms it. Let the rider edit or reject candidates.
8. Keep all recognition local and avoid uploading plates, GPS coordinates or images to online recognition services.

Possible TypeScript boundary (illustrative, not a verified library API):

export interface RecognizedTextCandidate {
  text: string;
  confidence?: number; // only if the underlying SDK supplies it
  boundingBox?: { x: number; y: number; width: number; height: number };
}

export interface TextRecognitionService {
  recognizeImage(localImageUri: string): Promise<RecognizedTextCandidate[]>;
}

Use an interface so ML Kit can be replaced by Apple Vision or a specialized ALPR model without rewriting the incident UI or persistence layer.

6. Suggested plate processing pipeline

ESP32 event + JPEG/image chunks
              |
              v
BLE assembly + incident image saved locally
              |
              v
Optional plate localization / crop
              |
              v
OCR (initially ML Kit; later dedicated plate OCR)
              |
              v
Normalize candidate text (uppercase, spacing, punctuation)
              |
              v
Check candidate length/format WITHOUT assuming one state's pattern
              |
              v
Show candidate + original image for user verification
              |
              v
Save reviewed result to incident SQLite record

Do not treat a pattern match as proof the text is a license plate. Characters such as O/0, I/1 and B/8 can be confused. State plate formats vary, and candidate identification should take nearby scene text and bounding geometry into account.

7. Proposed evaluation experiment

Use a reproducible set of at least 50–100 IBDC-like incident photographs, ideally from the actual ESP32 camera, covering:

• Stationary vs. moving vehicles
• Daylight, dusk and low light
• Clear vs. blurred plates
• Close vs. distant vehicles
• Different plate designs, orientations and partial occlusion
• Photos containing text unrelated to a license plate
• Frames with no visible plate (important negative cases)

Manually label a ground-truth plate string where it is actually legible, and label unreadable or no plate otherwise. Do not infer the true string from an external lookup when pixels are insufficient.

Measure:

|Metric                      |What it answers                                            |
|----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|
|Exact-plate accuracy        |How often is the entire plate string exactly right?        |
|Character error rate (CER)  |How frequently are individual characters wrong?            |
|Plate detection recall      |How often does a visible plate get located? (ALPR stage)   |
|False positive rate         |How often is an unrelated text string presented as a plate?|
|Unreadable/no-plate handling|Does the model refrain from guessing?                      |
|Latency per image           |How long does processing take on actual target phones?     |
|Memory / bundle impact      |How much RAM and app storage does integration require?     |
|Offline reliability         |Does the feature work in airplane mode after setup?        |

Compare ML Kit on full images, ML Kit on manually cropped plates, and FastPlateOCR on the same crops. If desired, add FastALPR full-image detection + OCR as a desktop benchmark before attempting mobile conversion. Never compare desktop latency directly against phone latency as if they were equivalent.

Initial acceptance targets (team proposals, not published accuracy claims):

• All recognition works with network disabled.
• No blocking or loss of BLE image transfers.
• Every predicted plate remains editable and marked unverified until reviewed.
• Measure latency and accuracy before setting numeric targets.

8. Suggested implementation phases

Phase A — easiest proof of concept

☐ Collect labeled test photos from the IBDC device.
☐ Check a maintained static-image ML Kit React Native integration against installed Expo/React Native versions.
☐ Build an Expo development client, not Expo Go, with the selected native OCR integration.
☐ Add TextRecognitionService and run OCR on a stored local photo URI.
☐ Display candidate text on the incident detail screen, with an option to edit/confirm.
☐ Save result and review status locally in SQLite.
☐ Record exact-string accuracy, false positives and phone latency.

Phase B — improve plate-specific accuracy

☐ Compare full-image OCR with manually cropped plate OCR.
☐ Prototype FastPlateOCR on cropped plates; document the exact model and weight license.
☐ Evaluate whether a lightweight plate detector improves results enough to justify mobile porting.
☐ Handle unreadable plates explicitly and reject low-quality predictions.

Phase C — optional integrated ALPR

☐ Benchmark FastALPR on representative IBDC images.
☐ Check runtime and model dependencies for mobile compatibility and licensing.
☐ Port detector + OCR to a suitable Android/iOS inference runtime if benchmarks justify the effort.
☐ Re-run the phone-based accuracy, latency, memory and offline tests.

9. Final recommendation

Choose Google ML Kit Text Recognition first, because it is free, runs on-device on both mobile platforms, and best matches the existing React Native/Expo app’s need to analyze stored incident images. Its chief limitation is that it is general-purpose OCR and does not identify a plate region by itself.

Keep FastPlateOCR as the next candidate if the prototype struggles with license plate characters, and FastALPR as the reference for a full detection-plus-recognition pipeline. Apple Vision is useful as an iOS-only comparison or fallback. PaddleOCR and Tesseract are reasonable open-source alternatives but involve additional mobile integration work.

Decision remaining: Run representative IBDC photographs through the candidates; there is not yet evidence that any of these models will meet a particular license-plate accuracy or processing-speed requirement on the ESP32 image resolution and target phones.

